This vignette adds a real jurisdiction from nothing: Hamilton, New Zealand.
It follows the route a planner or analyst would take with a plan they have
not modelled before: read the plan, enter its rules in the Excel template,
prepare the parcels and the zoning, run capacity, check it, and ask a
question of it. The walkthrough was first done on 23 November 2026 for one
area and five zones; the rulebook was then built out to the whole plan
(25 November 2026), and the last section runs it over the whole city.

## 1. Read the plan

Hamilton's rules are in the **Hamilton City Operative District Plan**, read
in its online ePlan at the revision of 13 November 2026. Plan Change 12
(Enabling Housing Supply), Hamilton's response to the National Policy
Statement on Urban Development, was made operative on 20 December 2024 and
is part of that text. The walkthrough starts with the zones of one area,
Claudelands and Hamilton East: General, Medium Density and High Density
Residential (Chapter 4), and the Business 6 (neighbourhood centre) and
Business 2 zones (Chapter 6). The finished rulebook adds the rest in three
stages:

- **residential**: Large Lot Residential, Peacocke's Medium Density zone
  (Chapter 4A), the residential precincts, the Three Waters Infrastructure
  Capacity Overlay's density, the riverbank and gully hazard setback and the
  5 m setback from arterial roads;
- **centres and business**: Business 1 to 7, the Peacocke centres, the
  Central City precincts, the Knowledge, Industrial, Ruakura and Te Rapa
  North zones;
- **overlays**: built heritage, Historic Heritage Areas, Significant Natural
  Areas, and the High Flood Hazard Area of Plan Change 14 (Flooding).

For each zone, the rules that shape a building or count its dwellings were
written down with their rule numbers (the notes are in
`data-raw/hamilton/rules_notes.md` in the package source). In summary:

| | General Residential | Medium Density Residential | High Density Residential | Business 6 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Height | 10 m (4.2.5.4) | 18 m, 5 storeys (4.3.4.4) | 26 m (4.4.5.4) | 11 m next to General Residential, 18 m next to Medium Density (6.4.1) |
| Recession plane | 45° from 4 m, not to a road (4.2.5.5) | one or two units: 60° from 4 m, 45° to General Residential; three or more attached units: no plane on side boundaries within 20 m of the road, 60° from 6 m beyond (4.3.4.5) | only to other zones: 45° to General Residential, 60° from 6 m to Medium Density beyond 20 m (4.4.5.5) | 45° from 4 m to General Residential, 60° to Medium Density (6.4.2) |
| Setbacks | road 1.5 m, side and rear 1.5 m (4.2.5.6) | road 1.5 m, side and rear 1 m (4.3.4.6) | 1 m; parts over 11 m 4 m back (4.4.5.6, 4.4.5.14) | 0 m front; 1.5 m to a residential zone (6.4.3) |
| Coverage | 45% (4.2.5.2) | 50% (4.3.4.2) | 60% (4.4.5.2) | floor area ratio 2:1 or 3:1 (6.4.4) |
| Landscaping | 20% (4.2.5.3) | 20% (4.3.4.3) | 10% (4.4.5.3) | none at ground floor dwellings: none allowed (6.4.7) |
| Dwellings | two per site permitted, more restricted discretionary (4.2.3.1) | three per site permitted, more restricted discretionary (4.3.3.1) | three or more restricted discretionary (4.4.3.1) | above ground floor only (6.3, 6.4.7) |

Some rules need more than zoning. The 5 m setback from arterial roads needs
the road hierarchy: the council's road centre lines carry a class, and the
rulebook names the column (`morphology: road_class_field`), so each road
boundary is tagged with the class of the road in front and a setback can
name `minor_arterial_road`. The density rule applies only inside an
infrastructure overlay, and the hazard setback only near a gully: both are
overlays with their own layers. Rules that cannot be applied are not dropped:
they go in the rulebook's register of rules not modelled, with the direction
each would move capacity.

Two choices in the full rulebook are assumptions, not rules, and the
rulebook says so: the Central City has no maximum height, so 32 m (above
which new buildings take added design matters) is used; and industrial
zones use a 7 m storey rather than the 3 m default. Plan Change 14
(Flooding) was decided on 24 December 2025 and is under appeal; its High
Flood Hazard Area is applied as though in force, marked as proposed.

The plan's definitions matter too. Hamilton's density rules are "average
net site area" per unit, and net site area leaves out any entrance strip or
private way; its floor area ratio is also over the net area of the site. By
default plancap measures both on the whole site, as capacityengine does, so
the rulebook says otherwise (`morphology: density_area: net`,
`far_area: net`). On a rear site the access leg then gives no dwellings.

## 2. Enter the rules in the template

`rulebook_template()` writes an empty workbook with one sheet per kind of
rule and a `fields` sheet explaining every column. The rules above were
typed into it, a row per zone, a row per setback, each with its citation:

```{r}
f <- system.file("extdata", "hamilton_rulebook.xlsx", package = "plancap")
if (!nzchar(f)) f <- "../inst/extdata/hamilton_rulebook.xlsx"
z <- openxlsx2::read_xlsx(f, sheet = "zones")
z[z$method != "none", c("zone", "codes", "height", "storeys", "coverage_max", "landscaping_min", "far_max", "height_source")]
s <- openxlsx2::read_xlsx(f, sheet = "setbacks")
s[s$target == "MRZ", c("target", "kind", "boundaries", "distance", "angle_deg", "start_height", "above_height",
                       "within_frontage", "beyond_frontage", "adjacent_zones", "not_adjacent_zones", "variant")]
```

Medium Density's two sets of standards (one or two units; three or more
attached) are entered as two **variants**: each site is built under both and
takes the one that enables more. Business 6's residential share is a band by
storey, none on the ground floor:

```{r}
openxlsx2::read_xlsx(f, sheet = "bands")
```

The workbook reads back as a rulebook, checked against the schema and for
missing citations:

```{r}
rb <- read_rulebook(f)
rb
validate_rulebook(rb)
head(rule_table(rb)[, c("zone", "rule", "value", "applies_to", "source")], 12)
```

## 3. Prepare the parcels and zoning

Two layers come from their publishers, cut to a square of about 1.4 km over
Claudelands and Hamilton East:

- **parcels**: LINZ NZ Primary Parcels (CC BY 4.0), split into land parcels
  and road parcels;
- **zoning**: Hamilton City Council's District Plan Zones. The council asks
  that its data be attributed to it and that derived work say it is not the
  authoritative source: this is derived work, not Hamilton City Council's
  data. The zoning is not shipped with the package.

Overlay layers come from the council's map services: the infrastructure
overlay and precincts (Plan Change 12), the gully hazard area, Peacocke's
height overlay, heritage and natural areas (Plan Change 9), the flood hazard
model, and the road hierarchy.

The zoning layer needs four changes before plancap can use it, which is
typical of a council layer (`data-raw/hamilton/prepare.R`):

1. Zone names become the codes the rulebook's zone map expects (`codes` in
   the zones sheet).
2. Business 6's height and floor area ratio depend on the residential zone
   it adjoins, so each centre polygon is coded by its neighbour.
3. Two features are not zones: the Industrial Amenity Protection Area (an
   overlay stored with the zones, used as one) and the Transport Corridor
   Zone.
4. Some polygons overlap by slivers; each overlap is given to one zone.

The third was found the useful way. The first run left the Transport
Corridor Zone in, and `check_capacity()` failed its land check: the sites
added to twice the land in the parcels. That zone is a single polygon under
every other zone, so it split nearly every parcel in two. Roads come from
the road parcels, so the polygon is dropped.

```{r}
source("../data-raw/hamilton/prepare.R")
h <- hamilton_inputs("../data-raw/cache/hamilton")
table(h$zoning$zone)
nrow(h$parcels)
```

## 4. Run capacity and check it

```{r}
names(h$layers)
sc <- scenario("Hamilton, Claudelands", rb, parcels = h$parcels, roads = h$roads, zoning = h$zoning,
               layers = h$layers, centrelines = h$centrelines)
cap <- estimate_capacity(sc)
summary(cap)
check_capacity(cap)
```

The sites with no zone are land parcels inside the transport corridor
(once the corridor polygon is dropped, nothing zones them). They are
flagged, not dropped.

```{r}
plot_capacity(cap)
plot_capacity(cap, measure = "dwellings", fill = "height_band")
plot_constraints(cap)
plot_capacity_map(cap, grid = 100)
```

## 5. Look at one site

A Medium Density site near a General Residential boundary shows the
alternative standards at work:

```{r}
s <- sf::st_drop_geometry(cap$sites)
pick <- s$site_id[s$zone_key %in% "MRZ" & s$area > 600 & s$area < 900][1]
ex <- explain_site(cap, pick)
ex$site[, c("site_id", "zone_key", "area", "variant", "storeys", "floor_area", "dwellings")]
ex$setbacks[ex$setbacks$storey == 5, c("segment_id", "boundary_class", "setback", "rule", "source")]
plot_site(cap, pick, view = "axo")
```

## 6. Ask a question: permitted activities only

Capacity here counts restricted discretionary development where the plan's
standards bound it, as plancap's Auckland example does. A narrower reading
counts only what is permitted: two units per site in General Residential and
three in Medium Density. That is a rule change, so it is a scenario:

```{r}
permitted <- rb |>
  set_rule("zones.GRZ.density.max_dwellings_per_site", 2) |>
  set_rule("zones.MRZ.density.max_dwellings_per_site", 3)
sc_p <- scenario_change(sc, "Permitted activities only", rulebook = permitted)
cmp <- compare_scenarios(run_scenarios(scenario_set(sc, sc_p)))
cmp$pairs[[1]]$totals
plot_comparison(cmp, measure = "dwellings", type = "bars")
```

Floor area does not move (the envelopes are the same); dwellings fall to the
permitted count wherever the envelope holds more.

## 7. What is not modelled

```{r}
unmodelled_report(rb)[, c("rule", "likely_effect")]
```

## 8. The whole city

The same rulebook and preparation run over every parcel in the city
(`Rscript data-raw/hamilton/run.R city`, about two and a half hours on one
core). The result is read back here rather than recomputed:

```{r}
city <- readRDS("../data-raw/cache/hamilton/city/run.rds")$cap
summary(city)
check_capacity(city)
constraint_breakdown(city, by = "zone")
plot_capacity_map(city, grid = 250)
```

## A checklist for a new jurisdiction

1. Read the plan for each zone you need: height (and storeys), recession
   planes, setbacks by boundary, coverage, landscaping and impervious area,
   floor area ratio, density, the dwellings each activity status allows, and
   any alternative standards. Note every rule number.
2. Write a blank template with `rulebook_template()`, fill the `plan`,
   `zones`, `setbacks` and `bands` sheets, and the register of rules not
   modelled. Read it with `read_rulebook()`; fix what `validate_rulebook()`
   reports.
3. Get parcels (LINZ in New Zealand) and the council's zoning. Map zone
   names to the rulebook's codes; take overlays and non-zones out of the
   zone layer.
4. Run `estimate_capacity()` and `check_capacity()`. A land check that fails
   means overlapping zones or parcels.
5. Look at a few sites with `explain_site()` and `plot_site()` before
   trusting totals.
6. Put the rulebook (`write_rulebook()`) and the preparation script under
   version control, so the run can be repeated when the plan changes.
